          "answer": 1,
          "explanation": "Correct answer is B (Answer Key: Topic 2, Q32). Verified from SAToplam 2.0 Reading real exam compilation."
        },
        {
          "id": "satoplam-t02-q033",
          "skill": "Details Question",
          "domain": "Information and Ideas",
          "focus": "Locate and interpret specific details explicitly stated in the text",
          "source": "SAToplam 2.0 · Topic 2 · Q33",
          "passageTitle": "Details Question — Q33",
          "passage": "In her 1998 book Blues Legacies and Black\nFeminism, Angela Y. Davis bases her analysis in\npart on recordings of songs sung in the 1920s by\nGertrude \"Ma\" Rainey and Bessie Smith. Davis\nfocuses on how Rainey and Smith improvised\nthe lyrics—replacing the original lines with\nmischievous jokes and wordplay. Davis's work\nwas particularly labor intensive because in order\nto transcribe, or write down, the lyrics as\nRainey and Smith sang them, Davis had to\nlisten repeatedly to the vinyl recordings, which\nweren't very clear.",
          "prompt": "What does the text most strongly suggest about\nthe songs sung by Rainey and Smith?",
          "options": [
