        {
          "id": "satoplam-t02-q054",
          "skill": "Details Question",
          "domain": "Information and Ideas",
          "focus": "Locate and interpret specific details explicitly stated in the text",
          "source": "SAToplam 2.0 · Topic 2 · Q54",
          "passageTitle": "Details Question — Q54",
          "passage": "Optimal foraging theory (OFT) holds that\nanimals' foraging behaviors reflect cost-benefit\ntrade-offs that vary by species and with dynamic\necological circumstances. One such circumstance\nis lunar intensity, which Joel Brown and\ncolleagues found to be negatively associated\nwith foraging by Arizona pocket mice but\nDeborah J. Curtis and colleagues found to be\npositively associated with foraging by mongoose\nlemurs. This discrepancy is explicable in terms\nof OFT: the lemurs' greater reliance on vision\nmeans that higher lunar intensity benefits them\nmore than it benefits the mice.",
          "prompt": "Which choice best describes the finding made by\nCurtis and colleagues, as presented in the text?",
          "options": [
            "Increased lunar intensity is more beneficial\nfor mongoose lemurs than it is for Arizona\npocket mice.",
            "As lunar intensity increases, mongoose\nlemurs increase their foraging activity.",
            "During periods of high lunar intensity,\nmongoose lemurs increase their reliance on\nvision.",
            "As lunar intensity increases, Arizona\npocket mice reduce their foraging activity."
          ],
          "answer": 1,
          "explanation": "Correct answer is B (Answer Key: Topic 2, Q54). Verified from SAToplam 2.0 Reading real exam compilation."
        },
        {
          "id": "satoplam-t02-q055",
          "skill": "Details Question",
          "domain": "Information and Ideas",
          "focus": "Locate and interpret specific details explicitly stated in the text",
          "source": "SAToplam 2.0 · Topic 2 · Q55",
          "passageTitle": "Details Question — Q55",
          "passage": "The following text is from Thomas Mann's 1924\nnovel The Magic Mountain, translated by John\nE. Woods in 1995.\nThe story of Hans Castorp that we intend to tell\nhere—not for his sake (for the reader will come\nto know him as a perfectly ordinary, if engaging\nyoung man), but for the sake of the story itself,\nwhich seems to us to be very much worth telling\n(although in Hans Castorp's favor it should be\nnoted that it is his story, and that not every\nstory happens to everybody)—is a story that\ntook place long ago, and is, so to speak, covered\nwith the patina of history and must necessarily\nbe told with verbs whose tense is that of the\ndeepest past.",
          "prompt": "What does the text most strongly suggest about\nthe story of Hans Catrop?",
          "options": [
