          "source": "SAToplam 2.0 · Topic 2 · Q60",
          "passageTitle": "Details Question — Q60",
          "passage": "Radamés Cordero et al. have confirmed that\ntwenty mushroom species (including Coprinellus\nmicaceus and species from the genus Russula)\nmaintain subambient temperatures in both their\nfruited bodies and their mycelium (the root-like\nhyphae from which the fruited bodies grow),\nthough the benefits of this hypothermic\nbehavior are unclear. Noting that relative\ncoldness was more pronounced in fruiting than\nin nonfruiting sites of the mycelium—a\ndifference that persisted even after fruited\nbodies were detached—the researchers speculate\nthat thermoregulation in the mycelium may\ninfluence reproductive success.",
